# text = But he welcomed Jem at his house , as he would have done his father's son , whatever were his motives for coming ; and now and then admitted the thought , that Mary might do worse , when her time came , than marry Jem Wilson , a steady workman at a good trade , a good son to his parents , and a fine manly spirited chap -- at least when Mary was not by ; for when she was present he watched her too closely , and too anxiously , to have much of what John Barton called " spunk " in him .
